// This file is generated. It holds every attribute the profile names, which is
// the point: the table it replaces held thirty-eight of them, and measured
// against pydicom's corpus that left sixty identifying attributes carrying
// their original values in files the library reported as de-identified —
// Patient's Sex in 49 of 69 files, Patient's Age in 25, Patient's Weight in 14.
//
// Two of those matter more than their count suggests. Frame of Reference UID
// and Referenced SOP Instance UID were left intact, and either one re-links a
// de-identified file to the instance it came from. An anonymizer that misses
// them has not anonymized anything; it has only made the file harder to read.
//
// The profile gives some attributes a choice of action, written X/Z, X/D,
// X/Z/D or X/Z/U*: remove it, unless emptying or replacing it is needed to keep
// the object conformant to its IOD. Resolved here toward keeping the attribute
// and removing its content, which is what dcmtk and dicom-anonymizer also do —
// an object stripped of a Type 1 attribute is no longer a valid object, and an
// invalid object is not a safe one.
//
// Actions the profile does not name are not applied. An attribute absent from
// this table is left alone, because the profile is a floor and silently
// discarding data the standard did not ask about is its own kind of wrong.

